{
    "feedback": {
        "progression": "The flow from Python basics through pandas and cleaning into EDA, statistics, and finally SQL makes sense, and SQL coming after the pandas work is smart since pulling query results into DataFrames will feel familiar by then. My concern is pacing: Session 2 jumps straight from 'beginner' to 'intermediate' Python in a single session, and Sessions 5 and 6 pack all the statistics I've never learned into two back-to-back sessions. Given that I only know beginner-level Python right now, that feels fast.",
        "engagement": "Honestly, this looks a bit dry to me. Sessions 5 and 6 especially sound like heavy theory, and I know myself - I'll skim the long explanations and jump to the practice tasks, which means I might miss key concepts on distributions and p-values. I like that EDA and visualization come before the stats deep dive, since charting distributions first will make the theory land better. But there's no mention of projects, milestones, or anything to keep my streak motivation going across 8 sessions.",
        "personalization": "The sequencing actually fits my background well - I already know visualization at an intermediate level, so using EDA as a bridge into statistics plays to my strengths, and the 'simulation before formulas' framing in Session 5 suits my learn-by-modifying-code style. The big gap is SQL: my goal and profile say I need advanced SQL for the junior data scientist role, but it's crammed into the last two sessions with nothing after it to consolidate. Also, nothing here connects to my marketing analyst experience - I'd engage much more with datasets from campaigns, funnels, or customer data than generic examples."
    },
    "suggestions": {
        "progression": "Split the Python jump in Session 2 or add an intermediate checkpoint before Session 3, and consider inserting a short review/practice session between Sessions 5 and 6 so the statistics concepts have time to consolidate before hypothesis testing.",
        "engagement": "Add milestone badges or streak checkpoints at Sessions 3 and 6, and make sure every session leads with a concise summary card plus a hands-on exercise on a realistic dataset - ideally I get runnable code first and theory second, since that's how I actually learn.",
        "personalization": "Move SQL earlier or spread it across the path (e.g., basic SELECT queries right after pandas) so I can hit advanced SQL well before my one-year goal, and anchor exercises in marketing datasets - campaign performance, A/B test results, customer tables - to leverage my existing domain knowledge."
    }
}
